(Discrepancy 1 (NAM), companion: D1.vox's garbage value is not a fixed
 sentinel and not derived from the string's content -- it is the
 ADDRESS of that declaration's own string literal in the compiled
 binary. Proof: two declarations of the identical string "abc" print
 two DIFFERENT values (n1 vs n2 below), ruling out both "a hash of the
 content" and "a fixed constant"; the gap between each consecutive pair
 equals the PRECEDING string's byte length plus one (3+1, 3+1, 2+1,
 1+1), consistent with each string sitting immediately after the
 previous one in the binary's data and the printed value being that
 string's own address. Also reproduces for `boolean` (b1). (The
 reassignment form is separately confirmed NOT to reproduce this --
 see variables.md VAR-26/VAR-26.vox, "cannot assign text to 'n', which
 is a number".)
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D1b.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   4198488
   4198492
   4198496
   4198499
   4198501
)
a number called n1 is "abc".
a number called n2 is "abc".
a number called n3 is "42".
a number called n4 is "a".
a boolean called b1 is "totally different string here".
